#ROOM
Vnum     199
Name     &RDO NOT FLY THIS SHIP~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob~
Desc     Do not fly this ship it is controlled automaticly by the code. Do not
fly this ship it is controlled automaticly by the code. Do not fly
this ship it is controlled automaticly by the code. Do not fly this
ship it is controlled automaticly by the code.

Wayfinding bands identify this part of The Hague Interstellar Terminal in several common trade languages.
~
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     200
Name     A Small Shuttle~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     You stand inside a small shuttlecraft that flies between Earth's
The Hague Interstellar Terminal and An Orbitting Shipyard. There are several plastic
covered seats along the walls but passengers are mostly expected to
stand for the brief journey.
Type LEAVE to exit after the ship lands.

Tall pressure-rated windows give brief views of Earth-orbit traffic approaching the terminal.
~
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     201
Name     Serin Pluogus Tourbus Platform~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob~
Desc     This platform is reserved for the publicly funded tour shuttle, the
Serin Pluogus. The rest of the The Hague Interstellar Terminal is to the west and
north of here.  Around the rest of the platform you can view the
southern reaches of Directorate  City ( a rather inapropriate name as
Earth has been the governing seat  of Terran Union for over
20 years ). To the south tower the Scheveningen Arcology Ridge.
When the pluogus lands type BOARD PLUOGUS to enter.

Terran Union customs, transit and emergency markings are integrated into the terminal walls.
~
#EXIT
Direction east~
ToRoom    204
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 209 1 201
Reset O 0 203 1 201
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     202
Name     Shuttle Platform~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob~
Desc     This platform is reserved for shuttles flying between The Hague Interstellar Terminal
and orbitting space stations. The rest of the The Hague Interstellar Terminal is to
the south-east and  north of here.  Around the rest of the platform
you can view the southern reaches of Directorate  City ( a rather
inapropriate name as Earth has been the governing seat  of the
Terran Union for over 20 years ). To the south tower the Scheveningen
Mountains.
 
Wait here for the shipyard ferry. When it lands BOARD FERRY.

Wayfinding bands identify this part of The Hague Interstellar Terminal in several common trade languages.
~
#EXIT
Direction west~
ToRoom    204
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 208 1 202
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     203
Name     Main Platform~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob~
Desc     This is the main platform in the The Hague Interstellar Terminal. All kinds of ships
are docked here. There are even a few that can be rented. The rest of
the spaceport is to the north. Around it is the southern end of
Directorate City ( or The Hague Government Quarter ) which covers a good portion of
the planet. To the south is a beautiful view of the Scheveningen Arcology
Range.

Tall pressure-rated windows give brief views of Earth-orbit traffic approaching the terminal.
~
#EXIT
Direction north~
ToRoom    204
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 206 2 203
Reset M 0 206 2 203
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     204
Name     Platform Area~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob~
Desc     From the platform area of the spaceport you have an excellent view of
the Scheveningen Arcology Ridge to the south. This large mountain range marks
the southern limit of The Hague Government Quarter. The air is cool here and
tastes cleaner than that  of the rest of the planet. It would be
quite peaceful here watching spaceships take off against the
backgound of the snow capped mountains. The main platform is to the
south of here. A walkway leads north into the spaceport.

Terran Union customs, transit and emergency markings are integrated into the terminal walls.
~
#EXIT
Direction north~
ToRoom    205
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction east~
ToRoom    202
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction south~
ToRoom    203
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction west~
ToRoom    201
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 204 2 204
Reset M 0 204 2 204
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     205
Name     Walkway~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     The walkway is a long corridor enclosed in transpariplast windows.
Through the windows you can see that the spaceport is surrounded by
mountains to the south and a seemongly endless city that stretches
out in all other directions.

Wayfinding bands identify this part of The Hague Interstellar Terminal in several common trade languages.
~
#EXIT
Direction north~
ToRoom    206
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction south~
ToRoom    204
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 210 2 205
  Reset E 1 332 1 5
  Reset G 1 3 1
  Reset E 1 202 1 8
Reset M 0 210 2 205
  Reset E 1 332 1 5
  Reset E 1 202 1 8
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     206
Name     Waiting Room~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     This large room is filled with benches and chairs. Most are arranged
in rows or around small islands of plants. Some however are arranged
in small groups  facing each other. Scattered amidst the seats are
many tables housing abandoned magazines. There is a projection display showing
an old movie in one corner of the room.

Tall pressure-rated windows give brief views of Earth-orbit traffic approaching the terminal.
~
#EXIT
Direction north~
ToRoom    209
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction east~
ToRoom    208
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction south~
ToRoom    205
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction west~
ToRoom    207
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 207 2 206
Reset M 0 207 2 206
Reset O 0 10315 1 206
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     207
Name     Spaceport Cafe~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     The spaceport cafe is a good sized self-serve cafeteria. There is a
long counter with many snack type foods and small instant meals. A
drink dispenser is at one end and at the other a robot teller handles
the credit transactions. There are a many tables along the walls and
a several in the center of the room.

Terran Union customs, transit and emergency markings are integrated into the terminal walls.
~
#EXIT
Direction east~
ToRoom    206
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 202 1 207
  Reset G 1 305 1
  Reset G 1 304 1
  Reset G 1 32207 1
  Reset G 1 201 1
  Reset G 1 204 1
  Reset G 1 205 1
  Reset G 1 206 1
  Reset G 1 207 1
  Reset G 1 208 1
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     208
Name     Lost and Found~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors donation~
Desc     The lost and found is a small plain room. There is a bin of clothing,
a table with several small boxes, a couple of large boxes and several
suitcases lined up against the wall. Some of the stuff has been
collecting dust for quite some time.

Wayfinding bands identify this part of The Hague Interstellar Terminal in several common trade languages.
~
#EXIT
Direction west~
ToRoom    206
#ENDEXIT

Reset O 0 210 1 208
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     209
Name     Spaceport Lobby~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     The The Hague Interstellar Terminal lobby is a large open room with a transparisteel
ceiling. The floor is a polished white with a black fractal pattern.
There is a ticket booth in one corner of the room. An escalater in
the opposite corner leads down.

Tall pressure-rated windows give brief views of Earth-orbit traffic approaching the terminal.
~
#EXIT
Direction north~
ToRoom    210
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction east~
ToRoom    212
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction south~
ToRoom    206
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction west~
ToRoom    211
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction down~
ToRoom    213
#ENDEXIT

Reset O 0 32201 1 209
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     210
Name     Spaceport Entrance~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     The entranceway of the Scheveningen Arcology Ridge Spaceport is a large wide
staicase  leading from the walkway outside down to the spaceport
lobby. A large set of double doors opens and closes automaticly as
people approach.

Terran Union customs, transit and emergency markings are integrated into the terminal walls.
~
#EXIT
Direction north~
ToRoom    301
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction south~
ToRoom    209
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 203 1 210
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     211
Name     Spaceport Hotel~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors safe hotel~
Desc     The The Hague Interstellar Terminal Hotel is attached directly to the lobby of
spaceport allowing travelers to find quick lodgings after a long
journey. The rooms here are well kept although a bit pricy. It would
be a good safe place to rest for a while.

You may safely leave and reenter the game from here.

Wayfinding bands identify this part of The Hague Interstellar Terminal in several common trade languages.
~
#EXIT
Direction east~
ToRoom    209
#ENDEXIT

Reset O 0 38 1 211
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     212
Name     Spaceport Store~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     The spaceport store seems to carry mostly only useless souveniers.
There are however a few nice garments for sale and one or two of the
things on display could come in handy if the prices weren't so high.

Tall pressure-rated windows give brief views of Earth-orbit traffic approaching the terminal.
~
#EXIT
Direction west~
ToRoom    209
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 201 1 212
  Reset G 1 32270 1
  Reset G 1 32261 1
  Reset G 1 32253 1
  Reset G 1 10424 1
  Reset G 1 10311 1
  Reset G 1 329 1
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     213
Name     Transit Station~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     The The Hague Interstellar Terminal Transit Station is one of many interconnected
maglev car stations running through The Hague Government Quarter. Its probably the
easiest fasted and cheapest way to get around the enormous city and
thus is a favoured means  of transportation.

Terran Union customs, transit and emergency markings are integrated into the terminal walls.
~
#EXIT
Direction east~
ToRoom    214
#ENDEXIT

#EXIT
Direction up~
ToRoom    209
#ENDEXIT

#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     214
Name     Toll Booth~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     For one small fee you can ride the maglev car transit system to almost
anywhere throught Directorate City. The turnstile allows one person
through at a time as they pay the robot on duty. The cost is 5
credits.

Wayfinding bands identify this part of The Hague Interstellar Terminal in several common trade languages.
~
#EXIT
Direction west~
ToRoom    213
#ENDEXIT

Reset M 0 200 1 214
#ENDROOM

#ROOM
Vnum     215
Name     The Hague Interstellar Terminal Station~
Sector   city~
Flags    nomob indoors~
Desc     This long narrow room is devided down the middle by a wide maglev car
track. The maglev cars move at extremely high speeds and as they
aproach a gust of wind tugs at you threatening to knock you off
ballance. When the maglev car comes to a complete stop you may "board"
it and look for a seat.

Tall pressure-rated windows give brief views of Earth-orbit traffic approaching the terminal.
~
#EXIT
Direction west~
ToRoom    213
#ENDEXIT

#ENDROOM

